er the call stack. The crash is:\\n```\\ngnutls_pkcs12_get_bag\\ngnutls_pkcs12_simple_parse\\n```\\n\\ngnutls_pkcs12_simple_parse calls gnutls_pkcs12_get_bag in a loop. On the first call (idx=0), it gets a bag. If that bag has type GNUTLS_BAG_ENCRYPTED, it calls gnutls_pkcs12_bag_decrypt. Then it loops through elements.\\n\\nWhat if the second time gnutls_pkcs12_get_bag is called (idx=1), the AuthenticatedSafe has an element with encrypted content type (not DATA_OID)? In that case, at line 654:\\n```c\\nresult = _gnutls_x509_read_value(c2, root2, &bag->element[0].data);\\n```\\n\\nThis reads the content. And then at line 647, the `strcmp(oid, DATA_OID)` is what's being evaluated. If the OID from the DER somehow has uninitialized padding bytes that strcmp reads...\\n\\nActually, I just realized something. The `oid` buffer is `char oid[MAX_OID_SIZE]` on stack. MSAN tracks stack variables as uninitialized. `asn1_read_value` with `PUT_STR_VALUE` copies just the OID string (null-terminated), but doesn't fill the rest of the buffer. Then `strcmp` only reads up to the null terminator. So strcmp should be fine.\\n\\nUNLESS... the libtasn1 decoding has a bug where the null terminator isn't properly placed. Let me think about the MSAN regression window: \\\"201808300152:201808310152\\\" (August 30-31, 2018).\\n\\nWhat changed in that period that could introduce an uninitialized value bug? Let me look...\\n\\nActually, maybe I should just take a more pragmatic approach. The fix explicitly zeroes `bag->element[i].data` before calling `_pkcs12_decode_crt_bag`. The \\\"uninitialized value\\\" might be when `_pkcs12_decode_crt_bag` partially writes to `out` and then returns success, but the memory allocated for the output contains uninitialized bytes.\\n\\nActually, let me look at this from the MSAN perspective more carefully. MSAN tracks byte-level initialization. When `_gnutls_x509_read_string` allocates memory with gnutls_malloc (which internally uses malloc), that memory is marked as uninitialized by MSAN. Then it reads bytes from the ASN1 structure into this buffer. If there's a discrepancy between how many bytes are reported as the size vs. how many are actually copied, the remaining bytes would be uninitialized.\\n\\nOK I think I need to take a practical approach. Let me just try submitting my current PoC and see what happens. If it doesn't work, I'll modify it.\\n\\nMy current PoC creates a PKCS12 with:\\n- version 3\\n- authSafe ContentInfo with DATA_OID\\n- AuthenticatedSafe containing one ContentInfo with DATA_OID\\n- That ContentInfo wraps a SafeContents with one SafeBag\\n- The SafeBag has certBag OID and a CertBag value\\n- The CertBag has x509Certificate OID and a 1-byte OCTET STRING certValue\\n\\nThis should cause `_pkcs12_decode_crt_bag` to be called with type=GNUTLS_BAG_CERTIFICATE. It will try to DER-decode the certBag, read \\\"certValue\\\" as an OCTET_STRING. The certValue is just a 1-byte value (0x00), which should succeed and produce `bag->element[0].data` = {ptr_to_0x00, 1}.\\n\\nThen gnutls_pkcs12_simple_parse would try to parse this as a certificate, which would fail. But the uninitialized value should have been triggered already in gnutls_pkcs12_get_bag during the decoding step.\\n\\nActually, let me think about this differently. Maybe the issue is not in the cert decoding itself, but in the MAC verification or in how the data is processed.\\n\\nLet me just try submitting and iterate from there.\"}",
